# Paste into /etc/ufw/after.rules, in the *filter block that declares
# :DOCKER-USER, immediately after the `-j ufw-user-forward` line and BEFORE the
# RFC1918 RETURN rules. Then `ufw reload`.
#
# Placement is the whole point: `-A DOCKER-USER -j RETURN -s 10.0.0.0/8` sits a
# few lines below and would accept this subnet's egress before any later rule
# could drop it.
#
# This lives here rather than only in the unit's ExecStartPre because `ufw
# reload` rebuilds DOCKER-USER from this file, which would silently discard a
# rule inserted by iptables at runtime -- reopening egress with nothing to show
# for it. The unit still inserts the same rule (idempotently) so a host that has
# not had this snippet applied yet is not left open.

# The container serves a game over a websocket and never needs to originate
# traffic. Intra-subnet is exempt so the published port's return path survives.
-A DOCKER-USER -j DROP -s 10.201.7.0/28 ! -d 10.201.7.0/28
